## What climate therapy is

Climate therapy is the therapeutic use of the local climate: the patient spends a prescribed part of the day outdoors — walking on marked paths, resting in the spa park or doing breathing exercises — and the spa physician determines how much, for how long and at what pace. In Mariánské Lázně (Marienbad) it is part of the treatment programme alongside the [mineral springs](/en/mineral-springs), natural carbon dioxide ([CO₂ therapy](/en/co2-therapy)) and peat ([peloid therapy](/en/peloid-therapy)); the climate is the town's fourth natural healing resource.

The town lies in a sheltered valley basin at an **altitude of 630 metres**, surrounded by the forests of the Slavkov Forest Protected Landscape Area. The local climate is described as **sub-montane and tonic** — mildly stimulating, without the temperature extremes of the lowlands. Since 2023 Mariánské Lázně has held the status of a **climatic spa**: the Czech Hydrometeorological Institute measured the air quarterly throughout the year from May 2021, and the values recorded lay below the limits set by Czech legislation. The status is state recognition of the climatic conditions as a natural healing resource; what a particular patient can expect from a stay is shown by the studies summarised below.

