## How climate therapy works

The spa physician prescribes climate therapy in three forms, which are usually combined during the stay.

{% treatment-box
   title="Terrain cure (climate path)"
   description="Medically prescribed walking on marked spa paths with a defined gradient, length and elevation gain. The physician sets the route, pace and length according to your state of health and adjusts them during the stay. Bring suitable shoes and clothing for changeable weather."
   icon="climate" /%}

### 1. Terrain cure

The core of climate therapy. The spa parks lead straight into the woods of the Slavkov Forest, and a network of marked paths begins a few hundred metres from the colonnade — from level promenades to forest routes with climbs. The physician chooses a route to match your current condition and increases the load in small steps; the pace, length and frequency of walks are set individually. Anyone who wants to find their own way around the terrain will find tips on the [day trips](/en/day-trips) page.

{% figure
   src="/images/trips/hamelika.jpg"
   alt="The stone Hamelika lookout tower among trees above the spa quarter"
   caption="The terrain cure follows marked paths of set gradient — through the forest up to the Hamelika tower, for one."
   credit="Photo: Lubor Ferenc · CC BY-SA 4.0"
   creditHref="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rozhledna_Hamelika_(3).jpg"
   width="wide" /%}

### 2. Open-air rest

Rest lying or sitting at the resting places and on the terraces of the spa parks, usually 30 to 60 minutes as prescribed by the physician. This is the passive form of climate therapy for patients with limited mobility or in convalescence, for whom the physician does not yet prescribe the terrain cure.

### 3. Outdoor breathing exercises

Practice of diaphragmatic breathing and prolonged exhalation with a physiotherapist, carried out on the terraces or in the park. The aim is to learn techniques the patient can go on using after returning home. For diseases of the respiratory tract, breathing exercises are usually combined with inhalations of mineral water from the [Forest Spring](/en/springs-overview).

## How long a course lasts

Climate therapy is prescribed for every day of the stay, as a rule one or two walks or one period of open-air rest a day. The length of the stay is determined not by a general rule but by the Czech indication list and the treating doctor's proposal: in comprehensive spa care covered by insurance it is 21 or 28 days depending on the item of the list, in contributory care 14 or 21 days (the specific lengths for individual diagnoses are in the overview below). Self-paying guests choose the length in consultation with the spa physician; shorter stays are regenerative rather than therapeutic in character.

## Which conditions it is used for

Climate therapy is not a separate item of the Czech indication list; the spa physician builds it into the treatment programme for these indication groups:

**Diseases of the respiratory system (group V).** Bronchial asthma and chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, conditions after complicated pneumonia, conditions after surgery of the respiratory tract, bronchiectasis and other items of the list. The terrain cure and outdoor breathing exercises form the exercise part of a programme that is complemented by inhalations and respiratory rehabilitation — more in the article on [post-Covid rehabilitation](/en/magazine/post-covid-rehabilitation).

{% indication-picker group="respiratory" /%}

**Skin diseases (group X).** Atopic eczema (X/1, basic stay 21 days in both comprehensive and contributory care) and generalised and arthropathic psoriasis (X/2, basic stay 28 days in comprehensive or 21 days in contributory care). Here the physician prescribes open-air rest and terrain walks as part of the daily regime alongside baths, peloids and phototherapy; the whole programme is described in the article on [spa treatment of skin conditions](/en/magazine/skin-conditions-spa-treatment) and on the page [skin diseases in the indication list](/en/indications-and-contraindications).

**As the exercise component of other indications.** On the basis of long spa practice, spa physicians also prescribe the terrain cure as controlled physical activity for diseases of the circulatory and musculoskeletal systems and in convalescence; an overview of all the diagnoses treated is on the [indications and contraindications](/en/indications-and-contraindications) page.

**Stress and exhaustion.** These are not items of the indication list; self-paying guests may choose a stay in a quiet setting as regeneration. A spa stay does not replace psychotherapy or psychiatric care — for long-term anxiety or depression, consult your doctor.

## When climate therapy is not suitable

The general contraindications of spa rehabilitation care apply: infectious diseases transmissible from person to person, all diseases in the acute stage (including an acute worsening of asthma or COPD), clinical signs of circulatory failure, malignant arrhythmias and persistently high blood pressure, conditions within 3 months of deep vein thrombosis, uncontrolled epilepsy, pregnancy and others — the complete list is on the [indications and contraindications](/en/indications-and-contraindications) page. For indication group V (diseases of the respiratory system), nicotine dependence is also a contraindication to covered care. The physician does not prescribe the terrain cure, or interrupts it, in cases of fever, breathlessness at rest, chest pain and an unstable condition; in such cases they choose passive open-air rest or postpone climate therapy.

## Who decides on inclusion

Whether climate therapy is included, and in what form, intensity and length, is decided by the **spa physician after the initial examination** on the day of arrival, on the basis of your medical records and current condition. During the stay the plan is adjusted according to how you tolerate the load. The spa paths and parks are open to all guests, but a walk becomes a therapeutic terrain cure only with a medical prescription.

## What a patient can realistically expect

Few studies exist for climate therapy at an altitude of around 600 metres; the closest evidence comes from comparable Central European spas and from research on inhalations and time spent in forests.

**Respiratory tract.** At a Hungarian spa centre in the Mátra mountains, which like Marienbad lies at a lower altitude (about 700 m), 678 patients with asthma, COPD or allergic rhinitis (mean age 61) completed a three-week programme of breathing exercises on outdoor terraces twice a day and rehabilitation exercise. The mean FEV1 — the volume of air exhaled in the first second — rose from 71 to 77.7% of the predicted value. The study had no control group, so the effect of the climate cannot be separated from that of the exercise and medical supervision ([Müller et al., 2018](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.eujim.2018.04.007), *Eur J Integr Med*).

**Inhalations.** Inhalations of mineral water are part of the programme for diseases of the respiratory tract. A systematic review of 27 studies of thermal mineral water inhalations described improvements in nasal patency, mucociliary clearance and some lung parameters; the quality of the studies was low and they mostly concerned waters other than those of Marienbad ([Fontana et al., 2025](https://consensus.app/papers/details/e377f688a5f85f3c9e6c9373d675eb8e/), *Int J Biometeorol*).

**Time in the forest and stress.** A meta-analysis of eight studies found lower salivary cortisol after time in a forest than in a city; this is a short-term effect in mostly healthy volunteers, and the authors point out that expectation may also play a role ([Antonelli et al., 2019](https://consensus.app/papers/details/9e2f3fee63dc533fa75d4391954c57d4/), *Int J Biometeorol*). What else the studies say about time in the forest is summarised in the article on [forest bathing in the Slavkov Forest](/en/magazine/forest-bathing-mindfulness-slavkov).

The realistic expectation, then, is this: regular guided walking and breathing exercises in surroundings free of dust and smog, which gradually increase the load under medical supervision and teach techniques that can be used at home as well. Climate therapy supplements the treatment of your pulmonologist, allergist or dermatologist; it does not replace it.

## The Slavkov Forest and the spa paths

The Slavkov Forest is a protected landscape area with extensive stands of spruce, pine and beech, without industry or through traffic. The spa woods above the colonnade are threaded with maintained paths with benches and resting places; north of the town, routes lead to the Kladská nature reserve with its peat bogs and a nature trail along a wooden boardwalk.

